description:
pkix-path-builder/src/lib.rs:85-92

`CertPool` implements `IntoIterator for &'a CertPool` (yielding borrowed certificates) but does not implement `IntoIterator for CertPool` (consuming the pool, yielding owned `Certificate` values).

Rust convention for collection types is to implement all three:
- `IntoIterator for &Collection` (already done)
- `IntoIterator for &mut Collection` (yielding `&mut Item`)  
- `IntoIterator for Collection` (consuming, yielding `Item`)

Without the consuming impl, callers who want to move certificates out of the pool after building (e.g., to pass them elsewhere) must call `pool.certs.into_iter()` — which doesn't compile since `certs` is private. They'd need to clone all certificates.

Fix: add
```rust
impl IntoIterator for CertPool {
    type Item = Certificate;
    type IntoIter = std::vec::IntoIter<Certificate>;
    fn into_iter(self) -> Self::IntoIter {
        self.certs.into_iter()
    }
}
```

The `&mut` impl is less urgent since there's no setter beyond `add`, but the owned consuming impl should be added for completeness.
